Workspace refactor delivery (@workspace-refactor: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @workspace-development) { r_6XJ2ZJ5ZVCANY0Z55SZ29XJ5GR
Use this Record for every [numbered stage](Workspace-Stages.lingua). The [workspace vision](Workspace.lingua) and [development graph](Workspace-Development.lingua) give the larger context.

## What ships with a stage

|> Every feature added or changed updates Laboratory → **showoff workspace** in the native desktop. Each workflow a stage claims must have a discoverable starting point, an action and a visible result in that workspace.

A button can expose a small operation. A Room with several Castles, a shader or a model can demonstrate a larger one. Make the parts usable individually and let people join them through the interactions the stage supports.

Keep building the same saved workspace. Preserve edits when an older showcase is reopened. Ship the fixtures or the means to build them inside Lince so someone with the binary can explore the result. Open it from Laboratory or launch the native desktop with `lince --showoff`. [Explore the shipped examples](../../fixtures/workspace/showcase/README.md) · [Showcase builder](../../../crates/interface/src/native/laboratory/showoff.rs)

## What proves completion

- Extend the cumulative end-to-end replay in a fresh profile. Use the shipped controls to build or change the part, try it independently and exercise its promised interactions with earlier exhibits. Verify the visible result and use pointer or keyboard input when testing whether a control is reachable. Keep earlier workflows passing. Reopen saved work in a fresh process when persistence is part of the claim. Run `cargo xtask workspace showcase <new-output-directory>` in the interface development shell.
- Run the stage’s focused unit and integration checks as well. Keep deterministic fault injection, scientific references and isolated performance measurements with their owning fixtures. Expose the user-visible recovery or result in the showcase when the stage promises one.
- In the stage handoff map each claimed workflow to its starting control or object, expected visible result and replay case. Include the interaction with an earlier exhibit when the objects can work together. Link the retained evidence from the [showcase guide](../../fixtures/workspace/showcase/README.md) so someone exploring a binary can repeat it. Mark the stage done after the cumulative replay, focused checks and stated metrics pass.
- Add a **Human required** line to a stage only when a necessary acceptance step genuinely depends on a person. Name the exact action or observation, why automation cannot establish it, and how to record the result. Routine exploration, UI input and unattended verification need no stage annotation.
